-- title:
--   §2, cyclic degenerate subgroup example
-- statement:
--   Let $d\ge1$, $H=\mathbb C^{\mathbb Z_d}$, and $Z=\mathbb Z_d\times\mathbb Z_d$. In the specified orthonormal basis let $W_{(x,y)}=U^xV^y$, where $Ue_k=e_{k+1}$ and $Ve_k=e^{2\pi i k/d}e_k$. Write $f_X(z)=\operatorname{Tr}(XW_z^*)/d$. All spaces are finite dimensional. The norm $\|X\|_2=\sqrt{\operatorname{Tr}(X^*X)}$ is the Hilbert–Schmidt norm, and $\nu_2(\Phi)=\sup_{\rho\ge0,\operatorname{Tr}\rho=1}\|\Phi(\rho)\|_2$ is the maximal output norm. A map in $W_1(H)$ is complex linear, Weyl covariant and satisfies $\Phi(I)=I$; it need not be positive or completely positive. Its computed symbol is $\phi(z)=f_{\Phi(W_z)}(z)$. Put $m(\phi)=\max_{z\ne0}|\phi(z)|^2$ and $E_{\max}(\phi)=\{z\ne0:|\phi(z)|^2=m(\phi)\}$. At $d=1$, explicitly set $m=0$ and $E_{\max}=\varnothing$. Write $S(\phi)=d^{-1/2}\sqrt{1+(d-1)m(\phi)}$.
--
--   The alternating pairing is $[u,v]=u_xv_y-u_yv_x\in\mathbb Z_d$. A subset is degenerate when this pairing vanishes for every pair of its elements. A maximal degenerate subgroup is inclusion maximal among degenerate subsets. For a unit vector $\psi$, define $G_\psi=\{z:\exists c\in\mathbb C,\ W_z\psi=c\psi\}$. Let $G(z)$ be the additive subgroup generated by $z$.
--
--   For every $z=(\alpha,\beta)$, $G(z)$ is degenerate. With residues represented in $\{0,\ldots,d-1\}$,
--
--   $$\gcd(\gcd(\alpha,\beta),d)=1\ \Longrightarrow\ |G(z)|=d.$$
--
--   In particular, for prime $d$, every nonzero $z$ generates a subgroup of order $d$. The nonzero guard in the prime specialization is required: the zero label generates only the identity subgroup. This supplies explicit maximal cyclic examples.
-- source:
--   On Weyl-covariant channels, arXiv:quant-ph/0510148v3, PDF p. 7, §2, cyclic degenerate subgroup example

